;;; 00e-standard-library.slat — Motoi Scheme Reference · Chapter 00e ·
;;; The standard library: parts you can include, exclude, and inspect.
;;;
;;; Written 2026-07-24, the day the standard was locked. This chapter is the
;;; programmer's tour of Motoi's standard library. It follows 00d-loading
;;; (eager vs lazy) and belongs to the intro: read it once and you know what
;;; the language ships with, where each verb comes from, and how to add your
;;; own part the same way the standard parts were added. Applies to Motoi,
;;; which inherits the same parts machinery.

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "Standard-library parts chapter — the reference audience is programmers, 11 to 111.")

(chapter :number "00e" :library "standard-library" :training-eligible #t
  :confidentiality "public"
  :read-order-hint "read after 00d-loading.slat, before 01-core.slat"
  :prose
"# The standard library — parts you can include, exclude, and inspect

## Who this chapter is for

This is the reference. The reference is for **programmers** — every one of them,
from an eleven-year-old writing a first loop to a hundred-and-eleven-year-old who
has written more code than you ever will. It tells you what the language gives
you, what each verb does, and how to reach for it. It does not tell you how the
runtime is *built* — that is a different book (the Scheme Engineering manual, for
people rebuilding or reasoning about the language from a language-engineering and
computer-science angle). Here, you are a programmer. You want to write a program.
This chapter tells you what is already in the box and how to add more.

## What Motoi ships with

Motoi's standard library is **R7RS-small plus a small set of named SRFI parts**.
That is the whole standard. No R6RS, no Racket extensions, nobody else's dialect.

- **R7RS-small** is the core — the official small-Scheme standard, the semantic
  authority. When a name like `map`, `cons`, `string-length`, or `assoc` means
  one thing in standard Scheme, it means exactly that thing here. This is the
  promise that lets anyone who already knows Scheme sit down and start typing.
- **SRFI parts** add the extra libraries the community standardized: lists,
  strings, hash tables, random numbers. Each SRFI is its own **part** — a named,
  numbered unit you can include or leave out.

The parts, as of the 2026-07-24 lock:

| part       | what it gives you            | mode  | in the box by default? |
|------------|------------------------------|-------|------------------------|
| R7RS-small | the core language            | eager | yes                    |
| SRFI-1     | the list library             | eager | yes                    |
| SRFI-13    | the string library           | lazy  | pulled in when needed  |
| SRFI-125   | intermediate hash tables     | lazy  | pulled in when needed  |
| SRFI-27    | sources of random bits       | lazy  | pulled in when needed  |

**The default bundle** is R7RS-small + SRFI-1. Those two are always on — the
\"expected superset\" every program can assume. The other three load lazily: their
verbs are there when you touch them, and they cost nothing until you do. (If you
have read chapter 00d, this is the same eager/lazy story, applied to the standard
parts.)

## Why the names match standard Scheme — the conformance ladder

Every verb in the standard library is held to the **highest standard it matches**,
in this order:

1. **R7RS-small first.** If the standard defines it, we use the standard name,
   the standard signature, the standard behavior. Full stop.
2. **Then a SRFI.** If R7RS-small does not cover it but a SRFI does, we conform to
   the SRFI.
3. **Then Motoi-own** — only if nothing standard matches. `random`, for example,
   is not in R7RS-small, so it follows SRFI-27.

This is why you will not find a verb here that *looks* like standard Scheme but
behaves differently. A verb that duplicated a standard one under a twisted name
or a different argument order would be a bug, and we fix bugs. One example worth
knowing: **folding**. Motoi uses SRFI-1's `fold` and `fold-right`, where the
element comes first and the accumulator second — `(fold cons '() lst)`. It does
**not** ship R6RS's `fold-left` or Racket's `foldl`/`foldr`. If you learned fold
somewhere else and the argument order surprises you, that is the conformance
ladder doing its job — see the broken-examples note at the end of this chapter.

## Every verb tells you where it came from

Open any standard part's spec and each verb carries its **provenance**: which
standard it belongs to and which section. `string-upcase` says SRFI-13.
`hash-table-ref` says SRFI-125. `list-tabulate` says SRFI-1. Nothing in the
standard library is anonymous. If you ever wonder \"is this real Scheme or did
Motoi make it up?\", the provenance answers you.

## Looking at the library from inside your program

The language can show you its own standard library. These verbs are always on:

```scheme
(std-parts)                 ; every standard part, with its metadata
(lib-info 'SRFI-13)         ; one part's metadata (name, spec, mode, verb count)
(lib-verbs 'SRFI-1)         ; the list of verb names a part provides
(lib-validate 'SRFI-125)    ; run the 5-point gate on one part, get a report
(lib-validate-all)          ; run the gate on every standard part
```

`(lib-info 'SRFI-13)` comes back looking like this:

```scheme
((:name \"SRFI-13\") (:std \"SRFI-13\") (:spec \"spec/srfi-13.slatl\")
 (:mode lazy) (:verb-count 15))
```

## Looking at it from the shell

The same information is a shell command away:

```
$ motoi lib list
R7RS-small   eager  116 verbs   spec/r7rs-small.slatl
SRFI-1       eager   25 verbs   spec/srfi-1.slatl
SRFI-13      lazy    15 verbs   spec/srfi-13.slatl
SRFI-125     lazy    19 verbs   spec/srfi-125.slatl
SRFI-27      lazy     4 verbs   spec/srfi-27.slatl

$ motoi lib info SRFI-13       # one part
$ motoi lib validate          # check every part against the running language
```

## Writing your own part — the same door the standard parts came through

Here is the good part: the standard parts are not special. They passed through a
gate, and *anything you write can pass through the same gate*. Write a library,
run the register command on it, and if it clears the gate it installs. If it does
not clear the gate, **nothing installs** — a broken part cannot dirty the language.

```
$ motoi lib register path/to/my-part.js
```

or from inside a program:

```scheme
(register-lib \"path/to/my-part.js\")
```

For a part to be accepted, its file must declare the same things every standard
part declares:

- `STD` — the part's name and provenance (e.g. \"SRFI-42\", or your own name).
- `SPEC` — the path to a spec file kept alongside the part.
- `VERBS` — the list of verb names it provides.
- `LAZY` — `true` or `false`: does it load on demand, or at boot?
- an `install…` function — the code that actually defines the verbs.

## The five-point gate — what \"accepted\" means

When you register a part, the language runs five checks. All five must pass:

```
$ motoi lib validate
PASS  SRFI-13
      ✓ 1-bucket:  part \"SRFI-13\" declares 15 verbs
      ✓ 2-spec:    spec present at spec/srfi-13.slatl
      ✓ 4-toggle:  LAZY=true (lazy, explicit)
      ✓ 3-verbs:   all 15 verbs bind to functions
      ✓ 5-runs:    installed surface is live (all verbs callable)
```

In plain words:

1. **Own bucket + provenance.** It is a real, named part with verbs, and every
   verb says where it came from.
2. **Spec kept alongside.** Its spec file exists on disk, next to the part.
3. **Every verb implemented.** Each verb the part promised actually binds to a
   working function.
4. **Includable / excludable.** It declares whether it is eager or lazy, and
   nothing else secretly depends on it being always on.
5. **It runs.** The installed surface is live — the verbs are callable, for real.

A part that fails any check is rejected, and the report tells you which check
failed, so you can fix it and try again. This is the whole promise: *write a lib,
run this on it, and it installs itself — but only if it is honest.*

## Broken on purpose — the teaching lane

You will find a folder of **broken examples** in the reference
(`scheme/reference/broken-examples/`). These are programs that do **not** run,
kept on purpose. Each one shows a mistake a Scheme programmer coming from another
dialect would naturally make — `fold-left` with the accumulator first (that is
R6RS, not us), `hash-table-exists?` for a membership test (that is the SRFI-69
spelling; the standard here is SRFI-125, which calls it `hash-table-contains?`) —
and then explains why it is wrong here, what the right Motoi way is, and how three
other Scheme libraries would each spell the same idea. They are not failures to be
cleaned up. They are the map of the differences between R5RS, R6RS, the SRFIs, and
Motoi. If you ever get a surprise, there is a good chance the surprise is already
written down there with its lesson attached.

## The short version

- The standard is R7RS-small + a few named SRFI parts. Nothing else.
- R7RS-small and SRFI-1 are always on. SRFI-13, SRFI-125, SRFI-27 load when you
  reach for them.
- Standard names behave the standard way, always — that is the conformance ladder.
- `(std-parts)`, `(lib-info …)`, `motoi lib list` let you look at the library.
- `motoi lib register` / `(register-lib …)` let you add your own part through the
  same five-point gate the standard parts passed.
- Broken examples are kept to teach the differences between dialects. Read them."

  :verbs (
    (:name "std-parts"        :library "standard-library"
     :signature "(std-parts) -> list-of-alists"
     :summary "Every standard-library part (R7RS-small + adopted SRFIs) as a list of metadata alists."
     :example "(length (std-parts)) ; => 5")
    (:name "lib-info"         :library "standard-library"
     :signature "(lib-info part-name-symbol) -> alist | nil"
     :summary "One standard part's metadata alist (name, std, spec, mode, verb-count), or nil if it is not a standard part."
     :example "(lib-info 'SRFI-13) ; => ((:name \"SRFI-13\") (:std \"SRFI-13\") (:spec \"spec/srfi-13.slatl\") (:mode lazy) (:verb-count 15))")
    (:name "lib-verbs"        :library "standard-library"
     :signature "(lib-verbs part-name-symbol) -> list-of-symbols | nil"
     :summary "The list of verb names a part provides, as symbols."
     :example "(lib-verbs 'SRFI-27) ; => (random-integer random-real ...)")
    (:name "lib-validate"     :library "standard-library"
     :signature "(lib-validate part-name-symbol) -> report-alist | nil"
     :summary "Run the 5-point gate on one standard part against the live language; return a report alist with :ok and per-check results."
     :example "(lib-validate 'SRFI-125) ; => ((:ok #t) (:std \"SRFI-125\") ...)")
    (:name "lib-validate-all" :library "standard-library"
     :signature "(lib-validate-all) -> list-of-reports"
     :summary "Run the 5-point gate on every standard part; one report per part."
     :example "(lib-validate-all) ; => reports for all 5 parts")
    (:name "register-lib"     :library "standard-library"
     :status "needs-alfred"
     :status-note "DRIFT (verified 2026-07-26): `register-lib` is NOT wired as a Scheme verb in the `run` env — `(register-lib \"lib/std/srfi-27.js\")` reports 'unbound symbol: register-lib'. It exists only as the JS function `registerLib` (src/lib-register.js:54); the neighbouring lib-info / lib-verbs / lib-validate / lib-validate-all ARE wired. DECISION NEEDED: (a) wire a `register-lib` Scheme verb over registerLib, or (b) reword this entry as a JS-API note rather than an in-language verb. The example does not run as written."
     :signature "(register-lib file-path-string) -> report-alist"
     :summary "Validate a candidate part file against the 5-point gate and, if it passes, install its verbs into the language. A part that fails installs nothing."
     :example "(register-lib \"lib/std/srfi-27.js\") ; => report with :ok #t")))
